  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Monday)\nIssued at 109 PM MDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\n19z surface analysis had sprawling high pressure over the Great \nLakes into the northern Plains bringing low-level easterly \ncomponent to boundary layer flow. Water vapour loop had upper low \nspinning over the SD/NE/IA/MN border area with backwash moisture \nspilling into the CWA. This moisture combined with weak upslope \nsupporting a few showers/drizzle over the area this afternoon, \nparticularly in/near Black Hills per latest KUDX radar loop. \nEffects of upper low exiting will be the main forecast concern \nmid-week.\n\nTonight through Wednesday, upper low shifts away with upper ridge\nexpanding into the northern Plains. Southwest flow aloft develops\nover the area with a few weak shortwaves expected to saunter \nthrough the CWA. First one arrives early Tuesday, helping increase\ngradient between developing lee trough and departing surface \nhigh. Two effects will occur. Breezy/windy spots will develop \nTuesday with increasing southeasterly winds. This will also \nsupport some fog/low stratus, especially east of the Black Hills. \nEarlier NBM guidance had bonkers wind gusts, but latest SPC \nHREF/NBM support a few gusts to 35-45mph from northwest into \nsouth-central SD with <20% chance of >45mph wind gusts. Other \neffect will be small clusters of PoPs southeast of the Black Hills\ngiven weak theta-e advection. Stronger wave moves northeast from \nthe central Rockies into MT Tuesday night/Wednesday. Effect of \nthis wave more pronounced with stronger low-level jet/theta-e \nadvection. This should promote some shra/TS moving southwest to \nnortheast over the CWA as MUCAPE slowly increases, especially over\nnortheastern WY. Temperatures will be near guidance and warmer \noverall.\n\nWarmer/drier weather expected the rest of the forecast period per \nsouthwest flow aloft and periodic low level downslope flow. The\nchance of precipitation is not zero, however.",
